<%# THE RUNWAY — this period as a line, with a mark on every day money is needed (two-shapes spec §3).
    Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ** IT IS THE PERIOD BAR'S SUCCESSOR AND IT ANSWERS A QUESTION THE BAR COULD NOT. ** The hero drew
    "day 7 of 14" and nothing else; the four dated rules falling due inside those fourteen days were
    scattered down the section below as clauses, so "is there anything coming?" took a scroll and a
    subtraction. Here the period is a ruler, today is a point on it, and every day money has to BE
    there is a point further along.

    ** ONE TICK PER DATED RULE DUE INSIDE THIS PERIOD, AND THE PERIOD IS `User#period_containing`. **
    No date arithmetic lives in this file: every position is `HomePresenter::Runway`'s own 1-based
    day over the same `Progress` the marker is drawn from, so a tick cannot be placed on a period the
    marker is not on. Absent entirely for a user who has declared no period — the refusal
    `#period_progress` has always made, because a runway is a picture OF a period and a calendar
    month nobody set would be thirty invented days.

    ** THE THREE COLOURS. ** Green is ready (the money is there), red is short (it is not, and the
    day is inside this period), and the olive — `--color-primary-dark`, the accruing colour — is the
    RAIL: the part of the period that has not happened yet. A tick is never olive, and that is this
    task's ruling: a rule due before the next period either has its money or does not, so "still
    accruing" is a sentence about a rule due LATER, which is where `HomeHelper#when_words` says it.

    ** THE LABELS ARE ON THE RAIL NOW, NOT IN A LIST BELOW IT (2026-09-06 layout ruling). ** They
    were stacked under the bar — the same ticks printed twice, once as marks and once as words — on
    the reasoning that two labels three days apart would collide at 375px. What that bought was a
    reader having to match a dot to a row by eye, and it is the wrong trade at full width: the panel
    spans the page now, so a fortnight is ~900px of rail and two labels have room to stand where
    their money is. Each tick is ONE element: the amount ABOVE the dot, the name and its state BELOW,
    both centred on the dot with `-translate-x-1/2` so the group's centre IS the day.

    ** A LABEL NEVER OVERPRINTS ANOTHER, AND THE WORDS ARE NEVER LOST (fix round 2 — MED-1/2). **
    The first cut assumed full width was room enough; it is not. Two bills on ONE day are 6px apart
    (the dot nudge does nothing for a 96px name) and two on ADJACENT days are ~75px apart at 1440,
    which is narrower than a name. So the tick loop carries a CLEARANCE — see it for the number and
    where the number comes from — and a tick too close to the last LABELLED one keeps its dot and
    goes `invisible`, held in place so the group stays symmetric about its dot and the dot stays on
    the rail. Nothing is lost: the moment any label is suppressed, the pace block's list appears at
    every width and says every tick's words in order. Below `sm` that list is always there and the
    rail carries amounts only, because at ~310px of rail no two labels fit.

    THE PANEL IS FULL WIDTH (see `index.html.erb`): the money is a row of tiles above it, not a
    column beside it, and a ruler is the one shape on this screen that spends width well. %>
<% if runway = presenter.runway %>
  <section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded p-4 lg:p-5" data-runway aria-labelledby="runway-heading">
    <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
      <h3 id="runway-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">This period</h3>
      <%# THE SAME SENTENCE THE HERO'S BAR CARRIED, and it is now printed once on this screen: the
          section below reads its heading off the blocks, not off the calendar. %>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500" data-period-days-left><%= pluralize(runway.days_left, "day") %> left</p>
    </div>

    <%# THE RAIL, INSET AND GIVEN AIR ON BOTH SIDES OF ITSELF. The horizontal padding is what a label
        centred on a tick at 0% or 100% overhangs into; the top padding is the amount's line, and the
        margin under the rail is the name's — both are absolutely positioned, so nothing below them
        is pushed and the clearance has to be stated. At 375 the names are invisible and the ends'
        dates come back up (`mt-3`). %>
    <div class="px-4 pt-7 sm:px-6">
      <%# The elapsed part is filled and the rest is the accruing olive; `role="progressbar"` because
          a bar with no accessible value is decoration to a screen reader, and the days-left sentence
          beside it is the value in words either way. The percent rides on the TRACK for
          `_this_period`'s reason — a 0% fill has no width and cannot be found. %>
      <div class="relative h-2 rounded-full bg-brand-dark"
           role="progressbar"
           aria-valuenow="<%= runway.percent %>"
           aria-valuemin="0"
           aria-valuemax="100"
           aria-label="Day <%= runway.day %> of <%= runway.days %> in this period"
           data-period-progress="<%= runway.percent %>">
        <div class="absolute inset-y-0 left-0 rounded-full bg-gray-300" style="width: <%= runway.percent %>%;"></div>

        <%# ** TWO MARKS CAN LAND ON ONE DAY, AND BOTH HAVE TO SURVIVE IT (fix round 1 — LOW-3). ** A
            12px tick drawn at the same `left:` as the 2px today mark covers it completely, and a bill
            due TODAY is the single most likely day for that to happen — the one afternoon the mark
            matters most. Two nudges, and neither is a colour trick:

              SAME-DAY TICKS STEP 6px EACH. `nudged_by` counts how many ticks have already been drawn
                on this day and offsets the next by half a dot's width, so two bills due on the 24th
                are two dots rather than one. It is a VIEW concern (pixels, not money), which is why
                it is counted here rather than carried on `RunwayTick`.
              TODAY IS DRAWN LAST, ABOVE, WITH A WHITE HALO. Later siblings paint over earlier ones in
                one stacking context, so the mark is below the tick loop and takes `z-10` as well —
                and `ring-1 ring-white` separates it from whatever colour it is standing on.

            ** AND THE WORDS NEED THE SAME RULING, WHICH THE NUDGE CANNOT GIVE THEM (fix round 2 —
            MED-1/2). ** 6px separates two DOTS; it does nothing for two ~96px names centred 6px
            apart, which simply overprint — and so do two ticks on ADJACENT days, because a day of a
            fortnight is about 75px of rail at 1440 and a name is wider than that. So the same pass
            carries `last_labelled` and a CLEARANCE, and a tick closer than the clearance to the last
            tick that KEPT its label is suppressed: its dot is drawn exactly as before, and its
            amount and name go `invisible`.

            `LABEL_CLEARANCE` IS 22 PERCENTAGE POINTS OF THE RAIL, and that is one spelling chosen
            deliberately over a per-breakpoint one. The bound is a ~110px label; the NARROWEST rail
            that renders labels at all is `sm` (640px viewport − 48 layout − 32 card − 2 border − 48
            rail inset ≈ 510px), and 110 ÷ 510 ≈ 21.5%, rounded up. Measuring against the narrowest
            means the rule is "never overprints at any width that shows a label" rather than "usually
            fits" — it costs some labels at 1440 that would have had room, and those words are in the
            list below, which is the trade this fix is making.

            INVISIBLE, NOT HIDDEN, AND NEVER ON A SECOND ROW. `visibility: hidden` keeps the group
            symmetric about its dot, so the dot stays on the rail (see the note on the name below);
            alternating labels between two rows would put the words at a height that no longer says
            which dot they belong to, which is the whole reason they came onto the rail. %>
        <% nudged_by = Hash.new(0) %>
        <% label_clearance = 22 %>
        <% last_labelled = nil %>
        <% any_suppressed = false %>
        <% runway.ticks.each do |tick| %>
          <% nudge = nudged_by[tick.day_index] %>
          <% nudged_by[tick.day_index] += 1 %>
          <% suppressed = !last_labelled.nil? && (tick.percent - last_labelled).abs < label_clearance %>
          <% last_labelled = tick.percent unless suppressed %>
          <% any_suppressed ||= suppressed %>
          <%# ** KEYED ON THE RULE, LABELLED SEPARATELY (fix round 1 — LOW-4). ** Two categories may
              each own an item called "Electric", and a hook keyed on the label answers for both — an
              ambiguous find in a spec and, worse, two marks a reader cannot tell apart. The id is
              the identity; the label is what it says.

              ** THE GROUP IS SYMMETRIC ABOUT ITS DOT ON PURPOSE. ** Amount and name are the same
              `leading-4` with the same `2` of margin, so the flex column's centre is the dot's
              centre and `-translate-y-1/2` lands the dot on the rail rather than near it. That is
              also why the name is `invisible` below `sm` and not `hidden`: hiding it would take its
              half of the column away and drop the dot off the rail. %>
          <div class="absolute top-1/2 flex -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 flex-col items-center"
               style="left: calc(<%= tick.percent %>% + <%= nudge * 6 %>px);"
               data-tick="<%= tick.line.rule.id %>"
               data-tick-label="<%= tick.label %>">
            <span class="mb-2 text-xs font-medium leading-4 tabular-nums whitespace-nowrap <%= "invisible" if suppressed %> <%= tick.short? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>"
                  data-tick-amount>
              <%= number_to_currency(tick.amount) %>
            </span>
            <span class="h-3 w-3 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-white <%= tick.short? ? "bg-status-danger" : "bg-status-success" %>"
                  title="<%= number_to_currency(tick.amount) %> <%= tick.label %>"
                  data-tick-mark="<%= tick.line.rule.id %>"
                  data-tick-label="<%= tick.label %>"></span>
            <%# NAME · STATE. The DAY is gone from the words and it is not missing: the tick's place
                on the ruler is the day, said in the one way a list could not say it. The state is in
                words as well as in colour, because a colour is not a sentence and a red dot with no
                word beside it is a screen asking to be interpreted. %>
            <span class="invisible <%= "sm:visible" unless suppressed %> mt-2 text-xs leading-4 whitespace-nowrap <%= tick.short? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-600" %>"
                  data-tick-name>
              <%= runway_tick_words(tick) %>
            </span>
          </div>
        <% end %>

        <%# TODAY, as a mark on the ruler rather than as the end of a fill: what is left of the period
            is the thing the ticks are placed in, so the day itself has to be findable. %>
        <span class="absolute top-1/2 z-10 h-4 w-0.5 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded bg-gray-900 ring-1 ring-white"
              style="left: <%= runway.percent %>%;"
              title="Today"
              data-today-mark></span>
      </div>

      <%# THE PERIOD'S OWN ENDS, labelled: the ruler is meaningless without them, and "Sep 1 — Sep 30"
          is where every tick's date gets its scale. %>
      <div class="mt-3 sm:mt-7 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 text-xs text-gray-500 tabular-nums"
           data-period-range>
        <span><%= runway.first.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
        <span><%= runway.last.strftime("%b %-d") %></span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# THE PACE LINE (§3), which is three sentences and never more, on ONE wrapping line:

          what a day may cost from here      — both signs of `free`, `HomeHelper#pace_words`
          what falls due before the period closes — Σ the ticks, or that nothing does
          which of them is short, by name    — absent when none is

        THE THIRD IS ABSENT RATHER THAN ZEROED: "0 rules are short" is a line that reports nothing.

        ** THE SECOND IS NOT (fix wave — Task 5's minor). ** It WAS absent on a quiet period, on the
        same reasoning — and a quiet period is the ordinary one, so the ordinary panel was a rail, a
        pace line and two-thirds of a card of white space at 1440. "$0.00 due before Sep 30" is
        indeed a figure pretending to be news; "Nothing is due before Sep 30" is an ANSWER, and it is
        the answer to the question this panel exists to ask. One sentence either way, in one place,
        so the panel has the same shape whether or not anything is coming.

        ** ONE LINE RATHER THAN THREE STACKED PARAGRAPHS (2026-09-06 layout ruling), joined by the
        middot the rest of this app separates clauses with. ** Each part is built with its own hook
        and its own colour and then `safe_join`ed, so the separator cannot appear before a part that
        did not render — which is what a hand-written "·" between two `if`s does on the first quiet
        period it meets. The trailing stop is dropped from each part, because a sentence ending in
        "." beside a "·" is two punctuations doing one job. %>
    <div class="mt-4 border-t border-gray-100 pt-3" data-pace>
      <%# ** THE TICKS' WORDS, WHEREVER THE RAIL CANNOT HOLD THEM. ** Below `sm` that is always: the
          rail is ~310px and two labels two days apart go through each other, so the amount stays up
          there (it is short, and it is the figure) and the names come down here, where a line may
          wrap.

          ** AND FROM `sm` UP, WHENEVER ANY TICK LOST ITS LABEL (fix round 2 — MED-1/2). ** The
          clearance above suppresses a label rather than overprinting it, and a suppressed label is a
          fact the screen has stopped saying — so the list appears at EVERY width the moment one is
          suppressed, and it lists every tick, not only the suppressed ones. Naming just the
          suppressed ones would read as a footnote about the odd bill out; the list is the same
          ticks in the same order, which is a thing a reader can check the rail against.

          `sm:hidden` ONLY WHEN NOTHING IS SUPPRESSED — that is the quiet period's panel, where the
          rail says everything and a list under it would be the same words twice. %>
      <% if runway.any_due? %>
        <ul class="mb-2 space-y-1 <%= "sm:hidden" unless any_suppressed %>" data-tick-list>
          <% runway.ticks.each do |tick| %>
            <li class="text-sm text-gray-700" data-tick-words="<%= tick.line.rule.id %>">
              <span class="font-medium tabular-nums <%= tick.short? ? "text-status-danger" : "text-gray-900" %>">
                <%= number_to_currency(tick.amount) %>
              </span>
              <%= runway_tick_words(tick) %>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      <% end %>

      <% parts = [] %>
      <% if pace = presenter.pace_line %>
        <% parts << tag.span(
             pace_words(pace).delete_suffix("."),
             class: pace.fine? ? "text-gray-700" : "text-status-danger font-medium",
             data: { pace_line: "" }
           ) %>
      <% end %>

      <% parts << tag.span(
           runway.any_due? ? "#{number_to_currency(runway.due_total)} due before #{runway.last.strftime("%b %-d")}" : "Nothing is due before #{runway.last.strftime("%b %-d")}",
           class: "text-gray-700 tabular-nums",
           data: { due_total: "" }
         ) %>

      <%# NAMED, ONE BY ONE, because "2 rules are short" is a fact the user cannot act on. The gap is
          `ClaimLine#fund_gap` — the same figure the trouble strip prints about the same rule. %>
      <% if runway.short.any? %>
        <% parts << tag.span(
             runway.short.map { |tick| "#{tick.label} is #{number_to_currency(tick.gap)} short" }.join(", "),
             class: "text-status-danger font-medium",
             data: { short_list: "" }
           ) %>
      <% end %>

      <p class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-sm">
        <%= safe_join(parts, tag.span("·", class: "text-gray-400", aria: { hidden: true })) %>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
<% end %>
